    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#mysore"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mysore.jpg" alt="Mysore" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#mysore">Mysore</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mysuru_Montage.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Sathyaprakash01, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mysore">Mysore</a> grew from a small chiefdom in the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#karnataka">Karnataka</a> plateau. According to dynastic tradition, recorded much later, a warrior named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#yaduraya">Yaduraya</a> founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#wadiyar-dynasty">Wodeyar</a> line at Mysore in 1399 after protecting the local ruling family, and his descendants governed a handful of villages as subordinate chiefs of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vijayanagara-empire">Vijayanagara Empire</a>. Little is securely known of the first two centuries, and the early chronology rests largely on these traditions. The decisive change came after Vijayanagara's defeat at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-talikota">Battle of Talikota</a> in 1565, which weakened imperial control of the south. Raja Wodeyar I (r. 1578–1617) took advantage of it, and in 1610 he seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#srirangapatna">Srirangapatna</a>, the island fortress on the Kaveri that had been the seat of Vijayanagara's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#viceroy-of-india">viceroy</a>, and made it his capital. He is also credited with beginning the royal celebration of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mysore-dasara">Dasara</a> festival at Mysore, presenting the Wodeyars as heirs to Vijayanagara's kingship. His successors expanded the kingdom across the southern plateau, absorbing neighbouring chiefs and resisting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#adil-shahi-dynasty">Bijapur Sultanate</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#madurai">Madurai</a> and other powers. By the late seventeenth century Mysore had become one of the principal successor states of Vijayanagara in the south, with an army and administration of its own.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#ranganathaswamy-temple-srirangapatna"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ranganathaswamy-temple-srirangapatna.jpg" alt="Ranganathaswamy Temple, Srirangapatna" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#ranganathaswamy-temple-srirangapatna">Ranganathaswamy Temple, Srirangapatna</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sri_Ranganathaswami_Temple,_Srirangaptna.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Prakruth Gowda, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mysore">Mysore</a>'s economy rested on agriculture on the dry plateau, where ragi and other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#millet">millets</a> were the staple crops, and on irrigated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rice">rice</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sugarcane">sugarcane</a> in the valleys of the Kaveri and its tributaries, watered by an extensive network of tanks and channels maintained by rulers and local notables. The forests yielded sandalwood, which became a valuable royal monopoly, and the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#malabar-coast">Malabar</a> in 1766 brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pepper">pepper</a>, cardamom and the ports of the west coast. Society was predominantly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hinduism">Hindu</a>, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#wadiyar-dynasty">Wodeyars</a> patrons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sri-vaishnavism">Srivaishnava</a> and Shaiva temples, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ranganathaswamy-temple-srirangapatna">Ranganatha temple</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#srirangapatna">Srirangapatna</a> and the Chamundeshwari temple on the hill above Mysore, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#jainism">Jains</a> and a growing Muslim population formed important communities. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kannada">Kannada</a> literature flourished at the court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#chikka-devaraja-wodeyar">Chikka Devaraja Wodeyar</a>. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hyder-ali">Hyder Ali</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tipu-sultan">Tipu Sultan</a> the state took a strong hand in the economy. Tipu established state trading enterprises, sought commercial privileges through embassies to the Ottoman sultan and to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#france">France</a>, regulated weights, measures and coinage, and promoted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sericulture">sericulture</a>, which became a lasting industry of the region. Revenue was assessed and collected directly by state officials as far as possible, reducing the share of intermediaries. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bengaluru">Bangalore</a>, a garrison and trading town, grew in importance, and Hyder Ali began the garden of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#lalbagh-botanical-garden">Lalbagh</a> there.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-pollilur-1780"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-pollilur-1780.jpg" alt="Battle of Pollilur (1780)" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-pollilur-1780">Battle of Pollilur (1780)</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rocket_warfare.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Charles H. Hubbell (1898-1971), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mysore">Mysore</a>'s early armies were raised from local chiefs and peasant infantry, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#wadiyar-dynasty">Wodeyars</a> fought frequent wars with neighbouring chiefs, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#madurai-nayak-dynasty">Nayakas of Madurai</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#keladi">Keladi</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#adil-shahi-dynasty">Bijapur Sultanate</a>. The kingdom became a major military power under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hyder-ali">Hyder Ali</a>, a cavalry commander who rose in the army during the 1750s. He built a large standing force that combined fast cavalry, infantry trained in European drill with the help of French officers, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#field-artillery">field artillery</a> and a rocket corps. Mysore's iron-cased rockets impressed British officers and influenced the later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#congreve-rocket">Congreve rockets</a> used by the British army. Hyder conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bednur">Bednur</a> in 1763 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#malabar-coast">Malabar</a> in 1766, and fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#first-anglo-mysore-war">First Anglo-Mysore War</a> of 1767–1769, ending it on favourable terms in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#treaty-of-madras">Treaty of Madras</a> after his cavalry appeared near the city. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#second-anglo-mysore-war">Second Anglo-Mysore War</a> (1780–1784) his army destroyed a British detachment under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#william-baillie">William Baillie</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-pollilur-1780">Pollilur</a> in 1780, though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#eyre-coote">Eyre Coote</a> defeated him at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#porto">Porto</a> Novo in 1781. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tipu-sultan">Tipu Sultan</a> continued the war after his father's death and concluded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#treaty-of-mangalore">Treaty of Mangalore</a> in 1784. Mysore also fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#maratha-empire">Marathas</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#nizam-of-hyderabad">Nizam of Hyderabad</a>, who became allies of the British in the Third and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#fourth-anglo-mysore-war">Fourth Anglo-Mysore Wars</a> that destroyed the kingdom.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#chikka-devaraja-wodeyar"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chikka-devaraja-wodeyar.jpg" alt="Chikka Devaraja Wodeyar" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#chikka-devaraja-wodeyar">Chikka Devaraja Wodeyar</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chikkadevaraja.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Artist unknown, photographer unknown, author: C. Hyavadana Rao, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#wadiyar-dynasty">Wodeyar</a> kings governed through a council of ministers and a <em>dalavayi</em>, or commander-in-chief, who could become more powerful than the king. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#chikka-devaraja-wodeyar">Chikka Devaraja Wodeyar</a> (r. 1673–1704) gave the kingdom a centralised administration, traditionally organised into eighteen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#departement">departments</a>, collected revenue directly through state officials and acquired <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bengaluru">Bangalore</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mughal-empire">Mughals</a> in 1689. In the eighteenth century real power passed to the ministers: from the 1730s the dalavayi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#devaraja">Devaraja</a> and his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#nanjaraja">Nanjaraja</a> dominated the court, and from 1761 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hyder-ali">Hyder Ali</a> took control of the government. Hyder never assumed the throne, and the Wodeyar kings remained nominal rulers. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tipu-sultan">Tipu Sultan</a>, who succeeded in 1782, went further, adopting the title <em>padshah</em> in 1786 and ruling as a sovereign. Tipu reorganised the provinces and revenue system, introduced a new calendar and new names for coins and weights, and kept detailed regulations for his officials and armies. His religious policies remain debated: he patronised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hinduism">Hindu</a> institutions, including the Sringeri monastery, which he helped after a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#maratha-empire">Maratha</a> raid in 1791, but he also deported the Catholic communities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mangalore">Mangalore</a> and carried out forced conversions in Coorg and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#malabar-coast">Malabar</a> during rebellions there. These policies left sharply divided memories that persist today.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#daria-daulat-bagh"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/daria-daulat-bagh.jpg" alt="Daria Daulat Bagh" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#daria-daulat-bagh">Daria Daulat Bagh</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gumbaj_containing_tomb_of_Tipu_Sultan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Rahulpurushot, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mysore">Mysore</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hyder-ali">Hyder Ali</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tipu-sultan">Tipu Sultan</a> was the most persistent opponent of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#east-india-company">British East India Company</a> in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#india">India</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#anglo-mysore-wars">Anglo-Mysore Wars</a> were central to the Company's rise to dominance in the subcontinent. Its military innovations, particularly its rockets, drew European attention, and examples captured at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#srirangapatna">Srirangapatna</a> were studied in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#britain">Britain</a>. Tipu's diplomacy, which reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#france">France</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>, showed an Indian ruler seeking allies in a wider world to resist the Company. His state-directed economic policies and administrative reforms are seen by historians such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#irfan-habib">Irfan Habib</a> as an attempt at modernisation, though others stress their limits. The kingdom's legacy is visible in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#karnataka">Karnataka</a> today: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mysore-dasara">Mysore Dasara</a> festival, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#silk">silk</a> industry of the region, the fortifications and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#daria-daulat-bagh">Daria Daulat Bagh</a> palace at Srirangapatna, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#lalbagh-botanical-garden">Lalbagh</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bengaluru">Bangalore</a>. The spoils of 1799 spread across Britain, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tipus-tiger">Tipu's Tiger</a>, a mechanical organ showing a tiger attacking a European, now in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#victoria-and-albert-museum">Victoria and Albert Museum</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#london">London</a>. In Indian memory Tipu became both a symbol of resistance to colonial rule and a controversial figure because of his treatment of some communities. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#wadiyar-dynasty">Wodeyar dynasty</a> survived the kingdom's fall and governed the princely state of Mysore until 1947.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#third-anglo-mysore-war"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/third-anglo-mysore-war.jpg" alt="Third Anglo-Mysore War" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#third-anglo-mysore-war">Third Anglo-Mysore War</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anglo-Mysore_War_3.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">No machine-readable author provided. Miljoshi assumed (based on copyright claims)., CC BY 2.5</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mysore">Mysore</a>'s fall came through war with an alliance it could not match. In 1789 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tipu-sultan">Tipu</a> attacked the lines of Travancore, an ally of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#east-india-company">East India Company</a>, provoking the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#third-anglo-mysore-war">Third Anglo-Mysore War</a>. Governor-General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#charles-cornwallis-1st-marquess-cornwallis">Lord Cornwallis</a>, allied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#maratha-empire">Marathas</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#nizam-of-hyderabad">Nizam of Hyderabad</a>, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bengaluru">Bangalore</a> in 1791 and besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#srirangapatna">Srirangapatna</a>, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#treaty-of-seringapatam">Treaty of Seringapatam</a> of 1792 Tipu ceded about half of his territory, paid a heavy indemnity and handed over two of his sons as hostages. He rebuilt his army and sought help from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#france">France</a>, which gave the Governor-General Richard Wellesley a pretext for war during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#french-revolutionary-wars">French Revolutionary Wars</a>. In 1799 British and Hyderabadi forces under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#george-harris-1st-baron-harris">General George Harris</a> invaded Mysore in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#fourth-anglo-mysore-war">Fourth Anglo-Mysore War</a>, and on 4 May 1799 they stormed Srirangapatna. Tipu was killed fighting near the walls. The Company annexed much of his territory, giving portions to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hyderabad">Hyderabad</a>, and restored the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#wadiyar-dynasty">Wodeyar dynasty</a> in the person of the four-year-old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#krishnaraja-wodeyar-iii">Krishnaraja Wodeyar III</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#purnaiah">Purnaiah</a>, Tipu's former minister, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#diwan">diwan</a>, over a reduced princely state under British protection. The Company took over its administration directly from 1831 to 1881. The princely state of Mysore joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#dominion">Dominion</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#india">India</a> in 1947, and its lands form the core of the present state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#karnataka">Karnataka</a>.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mysore">Mysore</a> matters as the Vijayanagara successor state that, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hyder-ali">Hyder Ali</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tipu-sultan">Tipu Sultan</a>, built a modernised army with rockets and French-trained infantry and fought four wars against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#east-india-company">East India Company</a>, becoming its most determined opponent in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#india">India</a> before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#srirangapatna">Srirangapatna</a> fell in 1799.</em></p>

